(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["cs-12-08"] = {"1":{"s":"A microcontroller is a computer on one chip: a CPU, memory (ROM and RAM), input and output ports, timers and often serial communication, all in one IC. It is made to control a machine or a device. It differs from a microprocessor, which is only the CPU and needs outside memory and I/O chips. Microcontrollers are cheap, small and use little power.","w":["A microprocessor is only a CPU; a microcontroller also has memory, ports and timers on the same chip.","Microcontrollers are used inside machines (embedded systems), not as general computers."],"x":["A microcontroller is a small computer on a single chip. It holds the CPU, ROM for the program, RAM for the data, input and output ports, timers and counters, and usually a serial port. It is designed to do one controlling job, such as running a washing machine.","A microprocessor like the 8085 needs memory and I/O chips outside, so a system built with it is larger and costs more. A microcontroller has them inside, so the system is smaller, cheaper and uses less power. The CPU is usually less powerful, as the job is simple.","Because the computer is hidden inside the machine, the system is called an embedded system."],"e":"A washing machine uses a microcontroller to read the buttons, run the timer, switch the motor and heater, and show the display."},"2":{"s":"The 8051 is an 8-bit microcontroller with a CPU, 4 KB of ROM, 128 bytes of RAM, four 8-bit I/O ports (P0 to P3), two 16-bit timers and counters, a serial port, five interrupts and a 16-bit program counter. It has an accumulator A, register B, four register banks of R0 to R7, a stack pointer, the data pointer DPTR, and the program status word with the carry and other flags.","w":["The 8051 has 4 KB of ROM, 128 bytes of RAM and four ports.","DPTR is a 16-bit register used to point to external data."],"x":["The 8051 is an 8-bit microcontroller made by Intel. On the chip are a CPU, 4 KB of program memory (ROM), 128 bytes of data memory (RAM), four 8-bit input and output ports named P0, P1, P2 and P3, two 16-bit timers and counters (Timer 0 and Timer 1), a serial port and five interrupt sources. It works with a 12 MHz crystal as typical.","The programming model: the accumulator A and the register B (used for multiply and divide); four banks of eight registers R0 to R7 in the RAM; the stack pointer (SP); the 16-bit data pointer (DPTR); the 16-bit program counter (PC); and the program status word (PSW), which holds flags such as the carry, the auxiliary carry, the register bank select bits and the parity.","The ports can be used for input and output to the outside, for example to read switches or to drive lights. The timers can count time or outside pulses."],"e":"To switch on eight lights, the program writes FFH to port P1 and the eight pins go high."},"3":{"s":"The 8051 family has other members with more features: the 8052 has 8 KB of ROM, 256 bytes of RAM and a third timer; the 8031 has no on-chip ROM; the 8751 has EPROM that can be erased and rewritten; and the 89C51 has flash memory. Other makers produce chips that follow the same instruction set. All are upward compatible with the 8051.","w":["The 8031 has no ROM on the chip and needs an external program memory.","The 89C51 has flash memory, which can be erased and programmed again."],"x":["The 8051 family is a group of microcontrollers with the same core and instruction set but with different memory and features. The 8031 has no program memory inside, so the program must be kept in an outside memory chip. The 8751 keeps the program in EPROM, which can be erased with ultraviolet light and written again. The 8052 is a bigger one with 8 KB of ROM, 256 bytes of RAM and a third timer.","Later chips such as the 89C51 use flash memory, which can be erased and programmed again electrically. Many makers, such as Atmel and Philips, made chips that run 8051 programs.","There are also other microcontroller families, such as the PIC and the AVR, but the syllabus asks only for the 8051 family."],"e":"A student learning with the 89C51 can program the chip many times on the same board, because flash memory is erased and written again electrically."},"4":{"s":"Microcontrollers are used inside many machines: household appliances (washing machines, microwave ovens, TV remotes), cars (engine control, anti-lock brakes), mobile phones, medical instruments, industrial control, toys, security systems and smart cards. They are chosen because they are cheap, small, reliable and use little power.","w":["A microcontroller is an embedded computer inside a device, not a general computer.","Know at least four applications from different fields."],"x":["In the home: washing machines, microwave ovens, air conditioners, TV and music system remotes and digital clocks. In vehicles: engine control, anti-lock brakes, air bags and dashboard displays.","In industry and offices: process control, robots, printers, photocopiers, scanners and card readers. In medicine: digital thermometers, blood pressure monitors and heart monitors. Others: mobile phones, toys, security alarms, ATMs, smart cards and traffic lights.","Microcontrollers are used because they are small and cheap, use little power, are reliable, and can be programmed for a particular job in a few kilobytes of code."],"e":"A digital thermometer uses a microcontroller to read the sensor, convert it to a temperature and show it on the display."}};
